{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1482759-arrow-of-artemis","id":1482759,"title":"Arrow of Artemis","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-06-02T08:01:10-07:00","id":1482759,"title":"Arrow of Artemis","content":"So, my friend thinks this gift (<i>Arrow of Artemis</i>, <i>Werewolf: The Dark Ages, page </i>103) <b><i>only</i> </b>causes 10 dice of aggravated damage, while I argue that this 10 dice of aggravated damage is in addition to the damage of the arrow itself, especially, because it would be a VERY weak gift if it caused only 10 dice of aggravated damage. I mean: Damn! It's a level 5 Gift! 10 dice of aggravated damage is definitely not worth it! <br /> What's your take on this?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-02T16:35:32-07:00","id":1482854,"title":"","content":"Is there still any warrior of Gaia around here? Or has the Wyrm prevailed?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-02T16:51:03-07:00","id":1482858,"title":"","content":"I\u2019m not an expert on W:tA, but that sounds like a lot of damage, especially if it\u2019s aggravated.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-02T17:24:37-07:00","id":1482865,"title":"","content":"Well, a normal bow would only cause between 3-6 dice of damage per the table of page 168 of the same book (depending on the bow type), so 10 aggravated damage is still quite an upgrade.<br /> <br /> The Gift does more than just provide damage. The user can see through any illusion or darkness that would impair sight (including any form of magic). And the person gets three bonus successes, and their normal die pool is at -2 difficulty.<br /> <br /> The Werewolf: the Dark Ages book does not cover the ordinary game mechanics because it is intended as supplement to the main book which I believe is the 2nd edition corebook. In that version, each success with Firearms provides an additional dice of damage. I would apply that to Archery (since it essentially replaces Firearms) as well which means the 3 bonus successes and the lower to hit difficulty will likely add more damage to the 10 dice of the Gift even after Dodge removes some successes.<br /> <br /> Seems OK to me, but since I haven't played with this rule I can't determine if it actually seems underpowered at the game table.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-02T17:26:42-07:00","id":1482866,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Amethyst</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1482759-arrow-of-artemis?p=1482858#post1482858\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I\u2019m not an expert on W:tA, but that sounds like a lot of damage, especially if it\u2019s aggravated.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> 10 <i>dice</i> of damage isn't really that much damage in Werewolf, it's a Rank 5 gift! Also, one can cause more than that with an average arrow or even better, with other lower level gifts that affect arrows, like <i>True Shot</i> that is btw a level 1 gift.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-03T03:36:05-07:00","id":1482916,"title":"","content":"Since the ability to scan for enemies is tied to actually firing the arrow and the Gift needs actually two turns (one action to charge the arrow, but since it costs Gnosis no Rage actions this turn) and one to fire the arrow. Since the damage is aggravated, but not mentioned as &quot;unsoakable&quot; I would think, that the successes from the roll to hit are carried over to the damage.<br /> <br /> 10 dice are about 5 successes, with a Stamina of 5 the damage would be 2 to 3 levels of agg damage on average. Hardly sufficient damage inflicted on an enemy that &quot;need slaying&quot;. With automatic successes and reduced difficulty to hit, the damage the damage is easily going up to 14 to 18 dices, which would be deadly on average.<br /> <br /> The other take would be, that the &quot;arrow inflicts 10 dice of aggravated damage.&quot; Period. Meaning, not soakable. In that case, the damage is relatively dangerous to most targets - a slightly above average damage roll will bring an enemy to &quot;Incapacitated&quot;, even without previous damage.<br /> <br /> Also, I think the corresponding Core Books would be Vampire: the Dark Ages. For it is a older Dark Ages supplement, not a WtA supplement given the page design..... <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-03T14:25:14-07:00","id":1482967,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I would think, that the successes from the roll to hit are carried over to the damage. </div> </div> </div>That's my impression as well, as otherwise I feel that the Gift wouldn't need to state that it gives <i>automatic</i> successes to hit, this, besides the fact that 10 dice of aggravated damage isn't even enough to kill a low tier vampire on average (and it's a damn rank 5 Gift lol).<br /> I'd rule it as the character rolls to hit and it adds to the damage (that will depend on the bow and arrow used, etc.) <b>AND </b>receiving the <i>bonus </i>of 10 dice of aggravated damage, <b>AS WELL.</b>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-03T14:53:09-07:00","id":1482971,"title":"","content":"Remember that it's a late 2e Gift from a crossover supplement... it wasn't the height of mechanical detail work over at the old WW. It's multiple editions out of date as it is.<br /> <br /> After dusting off my old books, I have to agree that the best interpretation of it is that the damage rating of the arrow is 10, and like most ranged weapons threshold successes are added on top of it for the total damage pool. Which, in the 2e rule set might not be the automatic insta-kill you'd want out of a level 5 Gift, but it's still a pretty nice power for 15 XP (remember Gifts tend to be cheaper than Disciplines and you don't have to buy them in order).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-03T15:07:35-07:00","id":1482972,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1482759-arrow-of-artemis?p=1482971#post1482971\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Remember that it's a late 2e Gift from a crossover supplement... it wasn't the height of mechanical detail work over at the old WW. It's multiple editions out of date as it is.<br /> <br /> After dusting off my old books, I have to agree that the best interpretation of it is that the damage rating of the arrow is 10, and like most ranged weapons threshold successes are added on top of it for the total damage pool. Which, in the 2e rule set might not be the automatic insta-kill you'd want out of a level 5 Gift, but it's still a pretty nice power for 15 XP (remember Gifts tend to be cheaper than Disciplines and you don't have to buy them in order).</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> It seems a sound interpretation, but I'd still take into consideration the bow and arrow used, as for dramatic purposes, such a gift should at least give the possibility of being an &quot;insta-kill&quot; one, what is not possible if you interpret it as only 10 dice of aggravated damage. It's such a pity it wasn't reworked at a later edition to avoid such hassle when interpreting it and also, this kind of gift is really fitting for a Black Furie and it adds to diversity. I remember an old friend of mine who used to complain that the WoD wasn't fond of archery, as there is a very limited number of &quot;bows and arrows things&quot; among the different splats.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-03T20:49:49-07:00","id":1483009,"title":"","content":"I think some of that is just a factor of the era of when the games were made. The mid-90s were not a great time for &quot;serious&quot; archer characters in a modern setting. Characters like Hawkeye and Green Arrow were rapidly shifted around between action comics with crazy gadget arrows and high melodrama non-action plots. The Hot Shots movies were actively using archery in modern action movies as farce. Etc.<br /> <br /> Today we have the far more grounded and relatable takes on fictional modern archers in things like the MCU (even making Hawkeye's array of specialty arrows work), Arrowverse, and Young Justice. <br /> <br /> Obviously Dark Ages games are a bit different on that, but the main books, where the majority of the rules and powers are found, were set in modern day at a cultural point were arrows were either for fantasy or comedy (intentional or otherwise)... so the rules didn't really emphasize them much.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-03T21:24:55-07:00","id":1483010,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1482759-arrow-of-artemis?p=1483009#post1483009\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think some of that is just a factor of the era of when the games were made. The mid-90s were not a great time for &quot;serious&quot; archer characters in a modern setting. Characters like Hawkeye and Green Arrow were rapidly shifted around between action comics with crazy gadget arrows and high melodrama non-action plots. The Hot Shots movies were actively using archery in modern action movies as farce. Etc.<br /> <br /> Today we have the far more grounded and relatable takes on fictional modern archers in things like the MCU (even making Hawkeye's array of specialty arrows work), Arrowverse, and Young Justice.<br /> <br /> Obviously Dark Ages games are a bit different on that, but the main books, where the majority of the rules and powers are found, were set in modern day at a cultural point were arrows were either for fantasy or comedy (intentional or otherwise)... so the rules didn't really emphasize them much.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Well, that's definitely a solid reasoning. I sincerely never thought of it this way, but then again, I was basically &quot;busy being born&quot; in the 90s, lol. <br /> Anyway, thanks a lot for the attention and the thoughtful answer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-05T00:41:34-07:00","id":1483110,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1482759-arrow-of-artemis?p=1482967#post1482967\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's my impression as well, as otherwise I feel that the Gift wouldn't need to state that it gives <i>automatic</i> successes to hit, this, besides the fact that 10 dice of aggravated damage isn't even enough to kill a low tier vampire on average (and it's a damn rank 5 Gift lol).<br /> I'd rule it as the character rolls to hit and it adds to the damage (that will depend on the bow and arrow used, etc.) <b>AND </b>receiving the <i>bonus </i>of 10 dice of aggravated damage, <b>AS WELL.</b></div> </div> </div> </div>I would think that the 10 agg damage dice replace damage dice stemming from bow and arrow (basically the weapon charts), but the normal rules for hitting a target apply, so net successes carry over.<br /> <br /> Two automatic successes to hit would make sense, even if they would not carry over, since it makes it so much harder to dodge the arrow (or parry with a shield or something like that).<br /> <br /> The gift is not talking about a 10 agg damage bonus, but just stating the arrow causes 10 dice of agg damage (so &quot;instead of what it usually does&quot;, is implyed, rather than &quot;in addition to what it susally does&quot;).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}